  │ [38;5;153mfalse[39m                     │ written correctly anyway.                                                           │
  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153msetUser[39m called with       │ Rejected with a warn rather than storing [38;5;153m"undefined"[39m — a stored garbage string      │
  │ empty/null                │ would read back as "logged in".                                                     │
  ├───────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Fresh tab, no login yet   │ [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m → [38;5;153mnull[39m, [38;5;153misLoggedIn()[39m → [38;5;153mfalse[39m.                                           │
  └───────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  The password never enters the session module and is never logged.

  [1mTesting and tooling[0m

  This repo has no test runner, no linter, and no dependencies — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block at all. [38;5;153mAppSession[39m is
  pure logic with branchy fallback behavior, which is the kind of thing that's cheap to test now and annoying to verify
  by hand later. Setting this up before the code exists is the cheapest it will ever be.

  Tests would need [38;5;153msession.js[39m to also export under CommonJS — one line ([38;5;153mif (typeof module !== "undefined") [39m
  [38;5;153mmodule.exports = AppSession;[39m) that changes nothing about how the browser loads it as a classic script.

  [1mMy recommendation: unit tests via the built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m, and skip the linter for now.[0m [38;5;153mnode:test[39m adds zero
  dependencies to a currently zero-dependency repo, which is the only reason I'd put a test runner into something this
  small. A linter here would mean adding the project's first dependency to police about 80 lines — I'd wait until
  there's more code to police.
